//Default Constructor
A default constructor is a constructor that takes no arguments.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student() {
        age = 20;
    }
};

Here:

Student()

is a default constructor because it takes no parameters.

When we create:

Student s1;

the default constructor is automatically called.

One important point

If you don't write any constructor, C++ can provide a default constructor automatically:

class Student {
public:
    int age;
};

Student s1;

Here C++ provides a default constructor implicitly.

But if you write your own constructor, C++ won't automatically provide the no-argument one for you.

Simple definition:

Default constructor = constructor that takes no arguments.

Exactly — the default constructor is itself a type of constructor. So there is no separate "use" of constructor vs default constructor.

Think of it like:

Constructor = general concept
Default constructor = constructor with 0 parameters
Parameterized constructor = constructor with parameters

For example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student() {
        age = 20;
    }
};

Here Student() is both:

a constructor ✅
a default constructor ✅
So why do we need constructors?

Because constructors let us initialize objects automatically.

For example, if every student should start with age 20:

Student s1;
Student s2;
Student s3;

The constructor automatically sets the age for each object.

And later, with a parameterized constructor, we can give different values:

Student(int a) {
    age = a;
}
Student s1(20);
Student s2(22);

So the hierarchy is simply:

Constructor
→ Default Constructor
→ Parameterized Constructor
→ Copy Constructor

We'll study each one by one.